Pointer arithmetic scales by the pointed-at size.

    long lbuf[4];  lbuf + 1     must be eight bytes on, not one
    long *q;       q + 1        the same
    int **p;       *p + 1       the same

micro-c emitted a plain add, so `p + n` advanced n BYTES whatever p pointed
at. Indexing was right -- p[n] scales, and so do `p++` and `p += n` -- which
is why this survived: this was the ONE arithmetic path that did not, and
M2-Planet's own sources index rather than add.

WHY THE SHELVED FIX DID NOT WORK, which matters more than the fix.

MICRO-C.md records scale_pointer_operand as written and not wired in, because
"it made two cases pass and broke a third". The stated reason was that
promote_type folds the pointee type away before the operator is handled. That
is true and it is not the whole story. Underneath it:

    primary_expr_number NEVER SET current_target.

An integer literal inherited whatever type the expression happened to be
carrying. So in `q + 1` the literal reported itself as `long*`, both operands
looked like pointers, and any rule keyed on "is the right operand a pointer"
read `p + n` as a pointer DIFFERENCE and scaled nothing. The shelved fix was
not seeing the operand types it was written against.

Four separate losses had to be closed, and each was invisible until the one
before it was fixed:

  1. common_recursion discards both operand types. It ends with promote_type,
     which is a NAME lookup -- `long* + int` comes back as plain `long`, with
     the indirection and the knowledge of which side carried it both gone.
     operand_left_type/operand_right_type record them one line earlier.

  2. An integer literal has no type. Fixed above.

  3. Indexing did not yield the element type unless `[`, `->` or `.` followed.
     `pn[0] + 1` on an `int*` therefore reported a pointer on the left, scaled
     the 1 by eight, and case 10 went red -- which is exactly the third case
     the shelved fix broke. The type now steps down whenever a value was
     actually LOADED, which is the honest test: `assign` is emitted for an
     rvalue and blanked for every lvalue context. The lvalue paths still need
     the pointer type because the store width comes from it, so this is keyed
     on the load rather than made unconditional.

  4. An array name used as a value is a pointer to its first element, and this
     compiler's type model cannot say so -- an array's current_target IS the
     element type, with the array-ness held on the symbol. `lbuf + 1` scaled
     nothing for that reason. NOT applied when a `[` follows, because
     postfix_expr_array takes its element width from current_target->size and
     decaying first would make `char s[10]; s[i]` stride by eight.

ALL FOUR SHAPES, because the previous attempt handled one and the other three
were not merely unhandled but actively wrong:

    p + n   scale the right operand by sizeof(*p)      result: pointer
    n + p   scale the LEFT operand, so swap first      result: pointer
    p - n   scale the right operand                    result: pointer
    p - q   subtract, then DIVIDE by the element size  result: integer

The swap goes through the stack rather than a scratch register: on aarch64 the
temps have a deliberately narrow M1 vocabulary (see emit_advance_temp) and this
is not on their list. The divide reuses the sequences the `/` operator already
lowers to on every architecture, so this adds NO new M1 macro anywhere --
checked against the patched tables for aarch64 and amd64.

pointer_element_size returns the size rather than a boolean, because "is it a
pointer" and "must I emit a multiply" are different questions: `char*` is the
first and not the second, and `p - q` on char* still yields a count and an
integer result. Conflating them is what the earlier version did. A FUNCTION
pointer and an opaque struct (size 0) are excluded -- neither has an element to
measure, and multiplying by zero would silently flatten the offset.

MEASURED, both architectures, patched tables, aarch64 under the committed
emulator:

    amd64     pass 45  fail 0    case 21 closed
    aarch64   pass 44  fail 1    case 21 closed; 16-switch-wide is PRE-EXISTING
                                 and fails identically without this patch
    regression.sh                0 regressions, 7 files compiled both sides
    tcc                          still builds; 369,440 lines of M1, 1.52 MB

AND IT DOES NOT MOVE tcc AT ALL. The marker trail with this patch is
byte-identical to the trail without it. MICRO-C.md called pointer scaling THE
BLOCKER; it was a real gap and it was not what tcc was stopping on. What tcc
was stopping on is the next patch in this series. The two are recorded
separately for that reason.

